GitHub is a web-based platform used for version control and collaborative software development. It is built on Git, a distributed version control system created by Linus Torvalds.

Key Features:

Repositories (Repos): Store project files and code.

Version Control: Track changes in code over time.

Branching & Merging: Work on new features without affecting the main project.

Pull Requests: Propose and review code changes.

Collaboration: Multiple developers can work on the same project.

GitHub Actions: Automate workflows like testing and deployment.

Why GitHub is Important:

Helps developers manage and organize code.

Enables teamwork and open-source contributions.

Acts as a portfolio to showcase projects.

Provides cloud backup for code.
